<article class="env-remark" id="04LP">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LP">Remark <span data-tag="04LP">9.6.2.6</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. It follows from Proposition <a data-tag="0043" href="/tag/0043">1.4.4.2</a> that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly $W$-local if and only if, for every morphism $w: C \rightarrow D$ which belongs to $W$, composition with the homotopy class $[w]$ induces a surjection $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( D, X ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}(C,X)$. In other words, the object $X$ is weakly $W$-local (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04LM" href="/tag/04LM">9.6.2.5</a>) if and only if it is weakly $[W]$-local when regarded as an object of the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04LH" href="/tag/04LH">9.6.2.1</a>). Here $[W] = \{  [w]: w \in W \} $ denotes the collection of all homotopy classes of morphisms which belong to $W$. </p>
